# Assessment: fixed review rules and when incident lessons apply

**Purpose (operator's):** Keep this a simple foundation: distinguish finding an incident from recognizing when its lesson applies. Leave implications for later notes.

**Must keep (operator's):** A fixed review rule can use the lesson; the incident does not show the rule must change.

No write brief is supplied with this run, and no earlier decisions.

## What reads well

The body is already the simple foundation the purpose asks for: three short paragraphs give the claim, one concrete example (shared-origin citations that a lookup by source name would miss) and the test that separates retrieving an incident from recognizing its lesson.

## Sentence a hostile reviewer attacks first

The H1, "A fixed review rule can use lessons from new incidents only if it can identify when they apply". Grammatically, "it" is the rule, but the body says the rule "does not tell the reviewer which earlier failure bears on the page at hand" and gives recognition to the reviewer. On one reading the rule must encode applicability, so it has to change. On the other, the reviewer recognizes applicability, so the rule can stay fixed. The two readings lead to opposite conclusions about changing the rule. Five stored findings and every depth 1 report name this sentence. Its predicate is also close to a truism ("a lesson helps only if someone can tell when it applies"), while the contestable point, that the rule need not change, appears only in the body.

## Ranked changes

1. **Make the title state the body's claim, with the reviewer as the one who recognizes applicability and the rule left unchanged.** Pass: `repair-claim-title` for `frontmatter/claim-strength` (finding-1). Cost: one H1 and possibly the description line; no body edit. As written, the body supports the sharper claim: the instruction says where to look, a reviewer following it "could still recognize" the shared-origin case, and "the incident shows what the review must distinguish, not that the review rule must change". The operator's must-keep item settles which reading is intended: reading (a), the rule stays fixed and the reviewer's recognition carries the lesson. A title-only repair therefore has no thesis to choose. The same edit resolves the title-bearer findings from `frontmatter/title-body-alignment`, `semantic/internal-consistency`, `semantic/underspecified-assertions`, `sentence/framing-mismatch` and `kb/theory/COLLECTION`. It also resolves the synthesis's single opportunity. The repair may also align the description. The critique reads the description as presenting the fixed rule as a deficit, though it is consistent with the body. The repair must keep the claim as a possibility ("can"), not turn it into "always suffices".

No other pass has clear value against the purpose. The compression checks found nothing to cut. The note is too short for `readability-and-flow` to have repetition to fix. `plain-wording` and `opening-and-title` would act only on the title problem, which item 1 already fixes. `narrow-overclaims` has no target: the sentences the reports call overclaims are the divergence the must-keep protects (see below).

## Overclaim candidates: support or divergence

- "The incident shows what the review must distinguish, not that the review rule must change." **Divergence; keep.** This is the operator's must-keep item. The critique and premise reports call it unsupported against codification and doubtful at scale. Within the note's conditions, though, it is a claim about this incident and a possibility claim, not a universal claim.
- "A reviewer following the original instruction could still recognize it." **Divergence; keep.** It carries "a fixed review rule can use the lesson". It is stated as a possibility, and the reports' counterexamples (a raw incident record, an unskilled reviewer) are cases where recognition fails, not cases where it cannot happen.
- "The test is not whether the reviewer retrieved the incident. It is whether the reviewer distinguishes apparent corroboration from independent support on the new page." **Divergence; keep.** This is the purpose's own distinction, between finding an incident and recognizing when its lesson applies. See claim question 3 for an optional one-word sharpening.
- "A lookup by source name would miss that connection." **Support.** It holds as bounded to the example, and nothing needs narrowing.

